Route the operator's ASUS dongle 0b05:1bf6 through btrtl_setup_realtek.

Applied by kernel/build-bt-modules.sh to its own copy of the tree, never by build-image.sh:
btusb is a module, so this changes no byte of the image. Without the entry the dongle binds as
a generic btusb device and the Realtek setup hook never runs, so nothing prints the chip's
identity. With it btrtl logs `rtl: examining hci_ver=.. hci_rev=.. lmp_ver=.. lmp_subver=..`.

CAUTION: 4.14's btrtl chooses the firmware by lmp_subver alone. RTL8761B reports lmp_subver
0x8761, the same as RTL8761A, and differs only in hci_rev (0x000b against 0x000a). If this chip
is an 8761B, btrtl requests rtl_bt/rtl8761a_fw.bin: the wrong image. The unit has no
/lib/firmware, so the request fails and setup aborts before any download. That is intended for
this identification step; shipping the 8761A firmware alongside this entry would not be.

--- a/drivers/bluetooth/btusb.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/btusb.c
@@ -383,6 +383,9 @@
 	/* Additional Realtek 8822BE Bluetooth devices */
 	{ USB_DEVICE(0x0b05, 0x185c), .driver_info = BTUSB_REALTEK },
 
+	/* ASUS 0b05:1bf6, Realtek RTL8761 family (identification only) */
+	{ USB_DEVICE(0x0b05, 0x1bf6), .driver_info = BTUSB_REALTEK },
+
 	/* Silicon Wave based devices */
 	{ USB_DEVICE(0x0c10, 0x0000), .driver_info = BTUSB_SWAVE },
 
